    "scenario": "MPLS L3VPN Hub and Spoke Route Targets",
    "goals": "• {{router2_name}}, {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} form an MPLS backbone in AS {{as_number}} where OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured, and the VPNv4 iBGP session between {{router2_loopback0_ip}} and {{router4_loopback0_ip}} is already established.\n    hub site: {{router1_name}}, Loopback1 {{router1_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router2_name}} over {{link1_cidr}}\n    spoke site A: {{router5_name}}, Loopback1 {{router5_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router4_name}} over {{link4_cidr}}\n    spoke site B: {{router6_name}}, Loopback1 {{router6_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router4_name}} over {{link5_cidr}}\n• Every customer router only has a default route towards its PE, and the three CE-facing PE interfaces are still in the global routing table.\n• Build a hub and spoke VPN with three separate VPN routing instances:\n    {{hub_vrf}} on {{router2_name}}, route distinguisher {{rd_hub}}, holding {{router1_name}}\n    {{spoke_a_vrf}} on {{router4_name}}, route distinguisher {{rd_spoke_a}}, holding {{router5_name}}\n    {{spoke_b_vrf}} on {{router4_name}}, route distinguisher {{rd_spoke_b}}, holding {{router6_name}}\n• Use exactly two route targets to enforce the topology: the hub instance exports {{rt_hub}} and imports {{rt_spoke}}, while each spoke instance exports {{rt_spoke}} and imports {{rt_hub}} only.\n• Reach each customer network with a static route inside its own instance towards the local CE, and advertise those routes into the VPN.\n• Result: both spoke sites must reach the hub site and the hub site must reach both spoke sites, but the two spoke sites must not reach each other, and neither spoke instance may hold the other spoke's prefix.",
